  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nBeginning Thursday, we begin to see a pattern shift, with the upper-\nlevel high pressure propagating east of the Panhandles. This will \nplace our CWA on the western periphery of the ridge in the \nsoutheastern US, where shortwave perturbations will provide better \nsynoptic scale support across the Panhandles. Beginning Thursday, a \nmoisture boundary is progged to setup across the southcentral and \neastern Panhandles, where current guidance is trying to initiate \nstorms across that boundary. Current conditions point toward the \nmain hazard of these storms being strong winds with DCAPE around \n1500-1800 J/kg. Forecasted soundings and forecasted surface \ntemperatures and dewpoints indicate inverted-V thermodynamic \nprofiles, which will likely lead to rain evaporating between cloud \nbases and the surface. While PWAT values dont point toward \ninefficient rain producers, moat precipitation will likely be \ncontained to the main core of any thunderstorms, meaning any \nlightning strikes that occur outside of the main core of a storm \ncould pose a fire start risk. This could be enhanced by any strong \nto severe wind gusts from storms and dry fuels, posing a risk for \nfire starts to spread. \n\nFriday and into the weekend, the upper-level high pressure \nestablishes itself southeast of the Panhandles. The anticyclonic \nflow and dry conditions of the high pressure to our southeast, will \nentrain dry air into the eastern Panhandles. This will shift our \nthunderstorm risk from the central and eastern Panhandles to the \nwestern and northwestern Panhandles as we head into the weekend. \nStorm coverage and severity for thee days are still an uncertainty, \nas only long-range models are seeing the event window so far. \n\nThe pattern is currently progged shuffle hot and dry conditions back \nin next week, as the upper-level high pressure retrogrades back \nwest. This will likely see the return of large scale subsidence and \nincreasing temperatures once again. \n\nFerguson",
